window.STUDY_CARDS = [{"id":"t.0-0","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"1. What is signal integrity and how do you maintain it in high-speed digital designs?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nSignal integrity is the ability of an electrical signal to travel from a transmitter to a receiver cleanly enough that the receiver samples the right logic level at the right clock edge—without reflections, crosstalk, overshoot, or ground bounce corrupting the waveform. In real post-silicon systems, signal integrity is inseparable from power integrity, because if the power delivery network drops during a heavy current transient, transistor switching slows down and noise margins shrink.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nHigh-speed edges contain high-frequency harmonics (f_knee ≈ 0.5 / t_rise), so PCB traces behave as transmission lines rather than simple wires. Whenever a trace encounters an impedance discontinuity—such as a via stub, socket contactor, package ball, or missing return-plane reference—part of the wave reflects back (Γ = (Z_L − Z₀) / (Z_L + Z₀)), causing ringing and inter-symbol interference (ISI) that closes the data eye. Simultaneously, rapid current steps (dI/dt) across package and board inductance L create inductive voltage droop (V_L = L · dI/dt) on the supply rail. Maintaining SI/PI requires: (1) controlled trace impedance (typically 50 Ω single-ended, 85–100 Ω differential) with proper ODT/series termination; (2) unbroken ground reference planes so return current follows directly beneath the signal trace; (3) tight length matching an\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Probe selection first: replace any long alligator ground lead with a < 5 mm spring-ground tip or active differential/power-rail probe so probe loop inductance doesn't create fake ringing.\n2. Measure rise/fall time, overshoot/undershoot, and eye width/height right at the receiver or nearest decoupling cap (not just at the VRM output).\n4. Correlate SI noise against simultaneous rail droop (AC-coupled 10–50 mV/div scope channel on VDD/VDDQ) to separate PDN-induced jitter from trace reflection.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-1","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"2. Explain the difference between single-ended and differential signaling.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nSingle-ended signaling sends one wire carrying the voltage relative to a shared ground plane, which makes it simple and pin-efficient but vulnerable to ground bounce and external noise. Differential signaling uses two tightly coupled complementary traces—positive and negative—and the receiver looks only at the voltage difference between them, so any noise coupled equally onto both wires cancels out.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn single-ended interfaces (like standard GPIO, SPI, or legacy CMOS clocks), the receiver threshold sits halfway between VDDIO and GND; if return currents shift the local ground potential by 100 mV, the effective noise margin drops by 100 mV immediately. Differential interfaces (like PCIe, USB3, SerDes, and LPDDR5 CK/DQS strobes) reject common-mode noise because V_diff = V_P − V_N. They also emit far less EMI because equal and opposite currents generate canceling magnetic fields, allowing smaller voltage swings (hundreds of millivolts instead of 1.8 V/3.3 V) at multi-gigabit speeds. The main failure mode of differential pairs is intra-pair skew (P and N trace lengths mismatched), which converts differential energy into common-mode noise and shrinks eye width.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n2. On a differential pair: use a matched active differential probe across P and N close to the receiver, plus single-ended checks on P and N separately to measure common-mode offset and intra-pair skew.\n3. Verify AC coupling caps (e.g., 0.1 µF/0.22 µF on PCIe TX) are present, same value on P and N, and not tombstoned.\n4. Check differential termination (100 Ω across P/N or 50 Ω to VTT) for excessive reflections.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-2","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"3. What is setup and hold time? What happens if these requirements are not met?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nSetup time is how long data must be stable before the capturing flip-flop's clock edge, and hold time is how long data must remain stable after that clock edge. If either is violated, the flip-flop can latch the wrong bit or enter metastability—hovering at an indeterminate voltage before settling unpredictably—which causes silent data corruption or random system hangs.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nMathematically, setup requires: T_clk ≥ T_clk_to_Q + T_comb_max + t_su − T_skew. Notice that clock period T_clk is on the left side—so lowering clock frequency (increasing T_clk) or raising VDD (reducing T_comb_max) gives you more setup margin. Hold requires: T_clk_to_Q + T_comb_min ≥ t_h + T_skew. Notice T_clk does not appear in the hold equation at all! Hold is checked on the same clock edge, which means lowering the frequency will never fix a hold violation; hold violations are fixed by adding buffer delay to fast paths, reducing clock skew, or adjusting PVT/corner timing.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run a Voltage-vs-Frequency shmoo plot: if the failure disappears when you lower frequency or raise VDD, it is a setup / max-delay path.\n2. If the failure persists at every low frequency (even 100 MHz) or gets worse at cold/fast-process corners, suspect a hold violation, race condition, or glitch.\n3. Check on-die rail droop with an AC-coupled scope channel during the failing workload—confirm VDD didn't dip below Vmin right before the failure.\n4. Inspect PrimeTime/STA corner reports or DFT scan-at-speed vs stuck-at results to isolate which clock domain or path is marginal.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-5","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"6. During SoC bring-up, how do you verify reset sequencing and clock readiness before BIOS/OS execution?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nEarly reset release can prevent reliable initialization, but a waveform alone does not identify the failing internal state. SYS_RESET# and PCIe PERST# have distinct requirements. Clock activity does not prove PLL lock. Check the selected device's power-valid thresholds, clock requirements and reset timing; do not substitute a universal delay or rail percentage.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nEarly reset release can prevent reliable initialization, but a waveform alone does not identify the failing internal state. SYS_RESET# and PCIe PERST# have distinct requirements. Clock activity does not prove PLL lock. Check the selected device's power-valid thresholds, clock requirements and reset timing; do not substitute a universal delay or rail percentage.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Use the four-channel overview above as a proposed probe plan. Measure rails with DC coupling when checking absolute level and slow droop; choose REFCLK probing, coupling and termination for its electrical standard. Use a suitable differential probe for a differential clock.\n2. Trigger on power enable or the selected reset edge and retain pre-trigger history. Choose record length and sample rate for both the complete sequence and the clock-detail view. A coarse overview cannot resolve individual high-frequency clock cycles.\n3. Measure each specified interval from its defined reference event. Use the device's power-valid threshold and clock-stability criteria, not an assumed 95% threshold. Compare the result against the applicable board/device or PCIe requirements.\n4. Check CPLD/TI UCD sequencer status registers over I2C/PMBus for any PG timeout or over-current fault flags.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-7","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"8. How is a DDR4/DDR5 interface different from LPDDR?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nDDR4 and DDR5 are designed for high-capacity, modular desktop and server systems using DIMM slots and higher signaling voltages, while LPDDR4/4X and LPDDR5/5X are designed for power efficiency and dense soldered-down or PoP integration in mobile and AI SoCs.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nDDR5 splits each DIMM into two independent 32-bit (+8-bit ECC) sub-channels to improve concurrency. LPDDR4/5 uses narrow 16-bit independent channels (often 4–8 channels per SoC), reducing activation energy per access. • Clocking & Features: LPDDR5 introduces WCK (a free-running or burst write/read clock running at 2× or 4× the command CK frequency), Link-ECC, Deep Sleep, and Partial Array Self-Refresh (PASR), plus dynamic DVFS frequency set-points (FSP) that require tight PHY ZQCal and training across PF states.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify the exact DRAM SKU/vendor (did ID in BL2/MRC logs) and confirm the matching dram_init / timing table is loaded.\n2. Check VDD/VDDQ/VPP rail levels and ripple against the JEDEC spec (e.g., 1.1 V DDR5 vs 0.5–0.6 V LPDDR4X/5 VDDQ).\n3. Inspect per-channel ZQCal PU/PD codes and MRC training pass/fail per byte lane across FSP/PF states.\n4. Compare 2D eye margins (SMV1D/SMV2D) at room vs hot (85 °C+ refresh/leakage) and cold (drive strength/training).","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-8","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"9. What are the key specs you consider when using an oscilloscope?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\n• Analog Bandwidth (−3 dB point): Must be at least 3–5× the highest fundamental frequency—or more accurately, governed by edge rise time: BW ≥ 0.5 / t_rise (10–90%). A 1 GHz scope distorts a 100 ps edge. • Sample Rate (GSa/s): Nyquist says > 2× BW, but for accurate waveform reconstruction without sin(x)/x interpolation artifacts on single-shot transients, you want ≥ 4–5× analog bandwidth. • Probe Bandwidth, Capacitance & DC Offset: A standard 10× passive probe with a 15 cm ground lead adds ~15 pF and > 100 nH of loop inductance—creating fake 100 MHz ringing! For mV ripple on a 1.2 V rail, use \n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\n• Analog Bandwidth (−3 dB point): Must be at least 3–5× the highest fundamental frequency—or more accurately, governed by edge rise time: BW ≥ 0.5 / t_rise (10–90%). A 1 GHz scope distorts a 100 ps edge. • Sample Rate (GSa/s): Nyquist says > 2× BW, but for accurate waveform reconstruction without sin(x)/x interpolation artifacts on single-shot transients, you want ≥ 4–5× analog bandwidth. • Probe Bandwidth, Capacitance & DC Offset: A standard 10× passive probe with a 15 cm ground lead adds ~15 pF and > 100 nH of loop inductance—creating fake 100 MHz ringing! For mV ripple on a 1.2 V rail, use a 1:1 / low-attenuation power-rail probe with built-in DC offset so you can zoom to 10–50 mV/div and use the full ADC resolution. • Memory Depth: Sample Rate × Time Window = Memory Depth. Deep memory lets you capture a 10 ms workload transition while\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify scope + probe combined bandwidth (system BW = 1 / √(1/BW_scope² + 1/BW_probe²)).\n2. Check input coupling & impedance: 50 Ω DC for high-speed clocks/SMA cables; AC coupling (or DC + offset on power-rail probe) at 10–50 mV/div for rail ripple/droop.\n3. Confirm probe attenuation factor in scope channel menu matches the physical probe (1× vs 10×) so voltage measurements aren't off by 10×.\n4. Check effective sample rate after setting horizontal timebase—make sure deep memory is enabled so sample rate doesn't silently drop.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-9","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"10. How do you use a DMM to measure current on a board?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nPrecision board shunts are typically 1 mΩ to 10 mΩ (e.g., 5 mΩ). Also remember a DMM integrates over tens of milliseconds—for fast dI/dt load steps, use a differential scope probe across R_sense or a clamp-on current probe.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nPrecision board shunts are typically 1 mΩ to 10 mΩ (e.g., 5 mΩ). Also remember a DMM integrates over tens of milliseconds—for fast dI/dt load steps, use a differential scope probe across R_sense or a clamp-on current probe.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Open the board schematic (e.g., Allegro) to find exact R_sense value, tolerance (e.g., 1% / 0.5%), and Kelvin sense test points.\n2. Zero/null DMM lead offset first (touch probes together in mV mode) because V_sense may only be a few millivolts.\n4. Cross-check calculated current against PMIC/VRM telemetry (I2C/PMBus READ_IOUT) to verify calibration.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-10","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"11. How would you validate a power rail with a DAQ setup?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA DAQ complements an oscilloscope: an oscilloscope captures nanosecond-to-microsecond AC transients, whereas a multi-channel DAQ (16–24 bit ADC) excels at long-duration, high-accuracy DC telemetry across 8–32 rails simultaneously over 24-hour thermal/Vmin soaks. Key electrical pitfalls when setting up a DAQ: (1) exceeding the DAQ's input common-mode voltage range (V_cm) when measuring a high-side shunt resistor on a 12 V rail; (2) ground loops when using single-ended DAQ inputs referenced to mains earth; and (3) aliasing if the rail has high-frequency switching ripple near the DAQ sample rate \n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA DAQ complements an oscilloscope: an oscilloscope captures nanosecond-to-microsecond AC transients, whereas a multi-channel DAQ (16–24 bit ADC) excels at long-duration, high-accuracy DC telemetry across 8–32 rails simultaneously over 24-hour thermal/Vmin soaks. Key electrical pitfalls when setting up a DAQ: (1) exceeding the DAQ's input common-mode voltage range (V_cm) when measuring a high-side shunt resistor on a 12 V rail; (2) ground loops when using single-ended DAQ inputs referenced to mains earth; and (3) aliasing if the rail has high-frequency switching ripple near the DAQ sample rate without hardware low-pass filtering.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify DAQ channel mode is Differential (not RSE/referenced single-ended) and within the DAQ's ±10 V / V_cm spec.\n2. Short each DAQ channel pair briefly at the bench to measure baseline noise floor and offset before connecting to the DUT.\n3. Cross-check the first DAQ reading against a calibrated handheld DMM at the exact same sense points.\n4. Log UTC timestamps alongside workload/thermal phase markers and flush CSV rows continuously so a 24-hour run never loses data.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-11","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"12. A USB 3.0 port works in USB 2.0 mode but not in 3.0 — what's your debug path?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nUSB 3.0/3.1 (5 Gbps / 10 Gbps) runs over SSTX± and SSRX± and performs RxDetect (the TX looks for 50 Ω far-end receiver termination through 0.1 µF AC coupling caps) followed by Polling.LFPS (Low-Frequency Periodic Signaling) and Gen1/Gen2 equalization training. If RxDetect fails—for example due to a missing/tombstoned AC coupling capacitor on SSTX, a bent SuperSpeed pin in the Type-A/Type-C receptacle, or a P/N polarity/TX-RX swap on the PCB—the port immediately falls back to USB 2.0 on D+/D−. Excessive parasitic capacitance from wrong ESD diodes or missing SuperSpeed redriver enable/strap pins\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nUSB 3.0/3.1 (5 Gbps / 10 Gbps) runs over SSTX± and SSRX± and performs RxDetect (the TX looks for 50 Ω far-end receiver termination through 0.1 µF AC coupling caps) followed by Polling.LFPS (Low-Frequency Periodic Signaling) and Gen1/Gen2 equalization training. If RxDetect fails—for example due to a missing/tombstoned AC coupling capacitor on SSTX, a bent SuperSpeed pin in the Type-A/Type-C receptacle, or a P/N polarity/TX-RX swap on the PCB—the port immediately falls back to USB 2.0 on D+/D−. Excessive parasitic capacitance from wrong ESD diodes or missing SuperSpeed redriver enable/strap pins can also kill LFPS or close the 5 Gbps eye.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Known-good A/B test: swap the USB 3.0 cable and peripheral first to confirm the issue stays on the DUT port.\n2. Linux check: run lsusb -t (verify 480M vs 5000M/10000M) and dmesg | grep -iE 'usb|xhci' for cannot enable port or SuperSpeed link-training errors.\n3. Microscope & continuity check: inspect the connector's inner SuperSpeed pins (pins 5–9 on Type-A, TX/RX on Type-C) and verify the 0.1 µF AC coupling caps on SSTX± are soldered and not tombstoned.\n4. Schematic & scope check: verify SSRX/SSTX routing isn't swapped at the connector/retimer, check retimer/redriver power & I2C config, and probe SSTX± for LFPS bursts during plug-in.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-12","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"13. How do you validate an Ethernet PHY at board level?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nMany board-level Ethernet failures happen in two classic places: (1) Strap pins sampled at reset de-assertion (determining PHY address, auto-negotiation, and RGMII internal clock delay—wrong pull-up/down values leave the PHY silent on MDIO or mis-timed); and (2) RGMII timing / MDI magnetics, where missing 2 ns TX/RX clock delay on RGMII causes packet loss at 1000 Mbps while 100 Mbps still works, or a mis-wired transformer center-tap bias breaks voltage-mode/current-mode MDI signaling.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nMany board-level Ethernet failures happen in two classic places: (1) Strap pins sampled at reset de-assertion (determining PHY address, auto-negotiation, and RGMII internal clock delay—wrong pull-up/down values leave the PHY silent on MDIO or mis-timed); and (2) RGMII timing / MDI magnetics, where missing 2 ns TX/RX clock delay on RGMII causes packet loss at 1000 Mbps while 100 Mbps still works, or a mis-wired transformer center-tap bias breaks voltage-mode/current-mode MDI signaling.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify PHY power rails, crystal/REFCLK frequency & jitter, and reset pulse timing relative to clock stability.\n2. Read PHY ID registers (0x02/0x03) via MDIO (phytool or firmware CLI) to confirm MDIO/MDC communication and strap address.\n4. Run PHY loopback + iperf3 stress across 100M/1G/10G; if 100M passes and 1G drops packets, check RGMII 2 ns TXC/RXC delay straps and MDI 100 Ω differential routing/magnetics.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-13","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"14. Walk me through how you'd bring up and test an I2C sensor.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nI2C uses open-drain drivers, meaning devices can only pull SDA/SCL low—external pull-up resistors (R_p, typically 2.2 kΩ–4.7 kΩ) are what pull the lines back high. If R_p is missing or too large for the bus capacitance (τ = R_p × C_bus), rising edges become rounded and fail setup/hold at 400 kHz Fast Mode. Other classic bring-up traps include 1.8 V vs 3.3 V level-shifter directionality, 7-bit vs 8-bit address confusion (0x48 vs 0x90), or a mid-transaction reset leaving a slave holding SDA stuck low (requiring a 9-clock recovery toggle on SCL).\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nI2C uses open-drain drivers, meaning devices can only pull SDA/SCL low—external pull-up resistors (R_p, typically 2.2 kΩ–4.7 kΩ) are what pull the lines back high. If R_p is missing or too large for the bus capacitance (τ = R_p × C_bus), rising edges become rounded and fail setup/hold at 400 kHz Fast Mode. Other classic bring-up traps include 1.8 V vs 3.3 V level-shifter directionality, 7-bit vs 8-bit address confusion (0x48 vs 0x90), or a mid-transaction reset leaving a slave holding SDA stuck low (requiring a 9-clock recovery toggle on SCL).\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Schematic & DMM check: verify VCC at the sensor pin, address pin strapping (ADDR0/ADDR1), and that both SDA and SCL sit idle-high at VDDIO.\n2. Linux bus scan: run i2cdetect -y <bus_num> and confirm the expected 7-bit hex address responds.\n3. Register read/write: read the Device ID / WHO_AM_I register via i2cget -y <bus> <addr> <reg> or Python smbus2.\n4. Scope check on SCL/SDA: verify 100/400 kHz clock frequency, clean ACK bit (9th clock low), and fast rise time without stair-stepping; compare sensor output against a reference instrument.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-14","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"15. How would you measure SoC power during idle vs. high load?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nTotal SoC power is P_total = P_dynamic + P_static = (α · C · V² · f) + (V · I_leakage(T)). Dynamic power scales linearly with activity factor α and frequency f, and quadratically with voltage V². Static leakage I_leakage grows exponentially with die temperature T! Therefore, comparing idle power at 25 °C vs 85 °C—or comparing high-load power across two boards without locking DVFS frequency and T-Case—produces invalid conclusions.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nTotal SoC power is P_total = P_dynamic + P_static = (α · C · V² · f) + (V · I_leakage(T)). Dynamic power scales linearly with activity factor α and frequency f, and quadratically with voltage V². Static leakage I_leakage grows exponentially with die temperature T! Therefore, comparing idle power at 25 °C vs 85 °C—or comparing high-load power across two boards without locking DVFS frequency and T-Case—produces invalid conclusions.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Lock thermal condition first (e.g., Chroma Cobra / MaxTC T-Case = 25 °C or 85 °C) and wait for thermal soak stability.\n2. For Idle: confirm OS/firmware entered deepest C-state/DVFS idle, kill background daemons, and integrate V_rail × (V_sense / R_sense) over 30–60 s.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-16","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"17. What's the typical DDR training sequence during boot?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nAt cold power-on, external DRAM cannot hold data until the Memory Reference Code (MRC) or DRAM initialization firmware calibrates the PHY interface to the physical board traces. The sequence runs in four main stages: first, DRAM reset and ZQ impedance calibration (ZQCL) against an external precision resistor; second, Command/Address (CA) training relative to the clock; third, Write Leveling and Read/Write DQS-to-DQ 2D eye centering; and fourth, locking the calibrated parameters and running a qui\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn modern SoCs, early boot runs out of tiny internal SRAM (BL2), which fetches a signed DRAM init binary (such as dram_init_7_usb_signed.bin / did7 matched to the DRAM vendor ID). The training FSM executes: (1) ZQCal (PCODE PMOS pull-up / NCODE NMOS pull-down) against a 240 Ω reference resistor so driver impedance matches PCB trace Z₀ (~40–50 Ω); (2) CA Training so Read/Write/Precharge commands are sampled cleanly; (3) Write Leveling & DQS-DQ Shmoo, sweeping picosecond phase delays and millivolt Vref (SMV1D/SMV2D) with checkerboard/PRBS patterns to map the 2D data eye; and (4) Eye Locking (Status 7 pass vs Status ff fail), which commits the DRAM address range to the NoC decoders and lets the CPU enable its MMU.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inspect early UART/BL2 boot logs for did vendor match, training stage reached (ZQCal, CA, WrLvl, RdEye, WrEye), and final status (7 vs ff).\n4. Compare SMV2D eye maps per byte-lane to see if a specific lane has collapsed width (timing) or height (Vref/noise).","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-18","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"19. How do you check PCIe link speed and width in Linux?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nPCIe devices advertise their maximum capability in LnkCap (2.5 GT/s = Gen1, 5 GT/s = Gen2, 8 GT/s = Gen3, 16 GT/s = Gen4, 32 GT/s = Gen5) and negotiate down during LTSSM Configuration/Recovery if signal integrity, lane continuity, or REFCLK fails—resulting in a lower LnkSta speed or narrower lane width (e.g., x8 instead of x16). One critical subtlety in Linux: PCIe ASPM (Active State Power Management) intentionally drops link speed to Gen1 when the GPU/endpoint is idle to save power, so always check LnkSta while the device is active!\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nPCIe devices advertise their maximum capability in LnkCap (2.5 GT/s = Gen1, 5 GT/s = Gen2, 8 GT/s = Gen3, 16 GT/s = Gen4, 32 GT/s = Gen5) and negotiate down during LTSSM Configuration/Recovery if signal integrity, lane continuity, or REFCLK fails—resulting in a lower LnkSta speed or narrower lane width (e.g., x8 instead of x16). One critical subtlety in Linux: PCIe ASPM (Active State Power Management) intentionally drops link speed to Gen1 when the GPU/endpoint is idle to save power, so always check LnkSta while the device is active!\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n2. Compare capabilities vs live link: sudo lspci -vv -s <BDF> | grep -E 'LnkCap:|LnkSta:|UESta:|CESta:'.\n3. For Python/Bash automation, read /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:<BDF>/current_link_speed and current_link_width under active workload (not idle).\n4. Check dmesg -T | grep -iE 'pcie|aer' for link-down, bandwidth-downgrade, or Replay/Receiver error bursts.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-20","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"21. A new board powers up but there's no boot log. What are your step-by-step debug actions?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA USB-to-UART bridge stays enumerated in Linux even when the target SoC is completely unpowered or stuck in reset.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA USB-to-UART bridge stays enumerated in Linux even when the target SoC is completely unpowered or stuck in reset.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n2. Power rails & sequencer: probe every VR output with a DMM against schematic tolerances; check TI UCD / PMIC PG flags.\n3. Clock & Reset: probe crystal/100 MHz REFCLK and SYS_RESET# on a 4-channel scope during power-on.\n4. Firmware & JTAG: check sha256sum of the flashed binary, inspect socket/flash seating, and test JTAG IDCODE scan.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-21","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"22. You suspect a missing clock signal — how do you confirm it?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA standard passive probe adds 10–15 pF of capacitive load. On a 24 MHz or 38.4 MHz crystal oscillator designed for 8–12 pF load capacitance, touching the crystal pin directly with a passive probe can either shift the frequency or stop oscillation completely—giving a false diagnosis! For differential HCSL/LVDS clocks (like 100 MHz PCIe REFCLK), probing only one leg misses common-mode or termination faults.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA standard passive probe adds 10–15 pF of capacitive load. On a 24 MHz or 38.4 MHz crystal oscillator designed for 8–12 pF load capacitance, touching the crystal pin directly with a passive probe can either shift the frequency or stop oscillation completely—giving a false diagnosis! For differential HCSL/LVDS clocks (like 100 MHz PCIe REFCLK), probing only one leg misses common-mode or termination faults.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Schematic check: confirm oscillator VDD, OE/enable pin polarity, and I2C clock-generator configuration (if programmable).\n2. Probe safely: use a low-capacitance active/differential probe (< 1 pF) at the receiver end of the clock line (past series resistors/AC caps).\n3. Scope measurements: verify exact frequency, Vpp/Vswing, 45–55% duty cycle, and monotonic edges.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-22","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"23. How would you troubleshoot an intermittent PCIe Gen4 link training issue?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA lower-speed pass shows speed sensitivity; it does not prove SI or exclude firmware, equalization, power or protocol causes.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nAt Gen4 (16 GT/s, 8 GHz Nyquist), channel loss must be evaluated at the relevant frequencies using channel measurements or models; it is not universally twice the Gen3 loss, so link training relies heavily on TX preset optimization (Phase 2/3 Equalization in LTSSM Recovery) and RX CTLE/DFE adaptation. Marginal socket contact resistance, bent pins, dirty gold fingers, riser impedance discontinuities, or excessive REFCLK SSC/jitter eat up the remaining eye margin, causing intermittent fallback to Gen3/Gen1 or narrow width (x8/x4) and Replay/Receiver errors.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Capture sudo lspci -vv -s <BDF> (LnkCap, LnkSta, AER Correctable/Uncorrectable) and dmesg -T | grep -iE 'pcie|aer'.\n2. Inspect PCIe gold fingers, riser connector, and SoC socket under a microscope for bent pins, debris, or flux residue.\n3. Perform controlled A/B swap (suspect DUT in known-good slot/riser; known-good DUT in suspect slot/riser).\n4. Force Gen3 speed (setpci or BIOS/root-port link cap) and run PCIe Lane Margining / SerDes eye scan to pinpoint which physical lane(s) are marginal.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-23","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"24. DDR validation fails under thermal stress but not at room temp — how would you debug?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nHot and cold failures have opposite physics: at hot (85–105 °C), capacitor leakage doubles roughly every 10 °C and transistor threshold shifts slow switching, shrinking tREFI retention and setup margins.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nHot and cold failures have opposite physics: at hot (85–105 °C), capacitor leakage doubles roughly every 10 °C and transistor threshold shifts slow switching, shrinking tREFI retention and setup margins.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Separate steady-soak thermal fails from ramp-rate fails; record both on-die sensors (memss/aoc) and T-Case/chamber RTDs with aligned UTC timestamps.\n3. Compare SMV2D eye shmoo at 25 °C vs failing temp and test cold-booting/retraining directly at the failing temperature.\n4. Check VDD_GMC and VDDQ_GMC rails at temperature for VRM thermal drift or increased dI/dt droop.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-25","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"26. A system crashes randomly — what logs or test methods would you use to isolate the root cause?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nRandom crashes usually fall into four buckets: (1) Power/Brownout (PMIC UVLO or dI/dt droop causing silent hard reset with zero OS kernel panic); (2) Thermal runaway/throttling; (3) Memory/Cache corruption (MCE, SError, ECC DBE); or (4) PCIe/Driver deadlock (AER Fatal, GPU Xid 79 fallen off bus). Knowing whether the OS printed a panic/Xid vs died instantly without a single UART byte immediately splits hardware power/reset faults from software/bus faults.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nRandom crashes usually fall into four buckets: (1) Power/Brownout (PMIC UVLO or dI/dt droop causing silent hard reset with zero OS kernel panic); (2) Thermal runaway/throttling; (3) Memory/Cache corruption (MCE, SError, ECC DBE); or (4) PCIe/Driver deadlock (AER Fatal, GPU Xid 79 fallen off bus). Knowing whether the OS printed a panic/Xid vs died instantly without a single UART byte immediately splits hardware power/reset faults from software/bus faults.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Recover previous boot logs: journalctl -b -1, /var/log/kern.log, /sys/fs/pstore/*, and external bench tail -F $AP_LOG.\n2. Check dmesg -T | grep -iE 'mce|aer|xid|ecc|panic|segfault|watchdog' and BMC/IPMI SEL (ipmitool sel list).\n4. If hard-hanging with zero OS logs, set a scope trigger on SYS_RESET# / PWRGOOD falling edge + core VDD to catch the hardware trip.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-26","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"27. Write a Python script to: Read voltage/current values from a DAQ, log them with timestamps, and plot power over time.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nCalling f.flush() per row, setting an explicit daq.timeout, converting SCPI strings immediately with float(), and closing resources in finally improves the example, but does not establish production readiness. On headless Linux servers without a display, matplotlib.use('Agg') ensures plotting never crashes on missing X11.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCalling f.flush() per row, setting an explicit daq.timeout, converting SCPI strings immediately with float(), and closing resources in finally improves the example, but does not establish production readiness. On headless Linux servers without a display, matplotlib.use('Agg') ensures plotting never crashes on missing X11.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run rm.list_resources() and daq.query('*IDN?') first to verify VISA driver and instrument address.\n2. Verify channel numbers (@101 voltage, @102 current/shunt) and scaling factor (1 / R_sense if reading shunt mV).\n3. Test with samples=5 first, inspect power_log.csv against a handheld DMM, and confirm power_plot.png renders cleanly.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-27","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"28. You're given a CSV log with performance and power — write a function to flag outliers.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nStandard z-score (|x − mean| / std > 3) works well for roughly normal telemetry, but one massive 100× spike in a log can inflate mean and std enough to mask smaller real outliers! Using MAD (median(|x − median(x)|)) alongside hard spec bounds makes outlier detection immune to extreme spikes, and checking .isna() catches instrument timeouts that logged blank cells.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nStandard z-score (|x − mean| / std > 3) works well for roughly normal telemetry, but one massive 100× spike in a log can inflate mean and std enough to mask smaller real outliers! Using MAD (median(|x − median(x)|)) alongside hard spec bounds makes outlier detection immune to extreme spikes, and checking .isna() catches instrument timeouts that logged blank cells.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inspect df.dtypes and df.isna().sum() first—string error tokens like 'ERR' in a CSV turn numeric columns into object dtype.\n2. Guard against zero standard deviation (std == 0) on constant idle rails so division never produces NaN.\n3. Unit-test with synthetic clean rows, injected spikes, and blank rows before running across fleet logs.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-28","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"29. How would you use Python to parse UART logs and extract boot failure messages?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nMulti-hour SLT soak logs can easily exceed several gigabytes. Calling f.read() or f.readlines() will blow up RAM, whereas iterating for lineno, line in enumerate(f, 1): uses constant O(1) memory. Compiling re.compile(...) once outside the loop avoids recompiling on every line, and keeping a small sliding buffer (collections.deque(maxlen=5)) lets you print the 5 lines right before a panic or Training FAILED message.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nMulti-hour SLT soak logs can easily exceed several gigabytes. Calling f.read() or f.readlines() will blow up RAM, whereas iterating for lineno, line in enumerate(f, 1): uses constant O(1) memory. Compiling re.compile(...) once outside the loop avoids recompiling on every line, and keeping a small sliding buffer (collections.deque(maxlen=5)) lets you print the 5 lines right before a panic or Training FAILED message.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Test against a real messy UART capture containing ANSI color codes or garbled reset bytes (errors='replace').\n2. Verify regex doesn't false-positive on benign boot lines like '0 errors detected'—add negative lookbehind or explicit pass-count exclusions if needed.\n3. Export hits to CSV/JSON with dut_id and timestamp so multi-bench failures aggregate cleanly.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-29","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"30. Describe a Python-based framework you've built or used for hardware validation automation.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nThe framework handled pre-flight serial/SSH and thermal checks, head-level locking so dual thermal heads never collided on commands, deterministic preconditions (LK mode, DRAM init, PF settings), structured CSV/UART logging, and automated JMP/JSL heatmap generation.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nMost importantly, every run wraps hardware control in try/finally safety hooks so a DUT hang or script interrupt automatically returns the thermal head to 25 °C idle and releases locks.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n2. Dry-run / unit-test parsers with pytest + mocked instrument/serial responses before deploying to 24-hour hardware benches.\n3. Inspect per-run artifact folder (raw_uart.log, thermal.csv, summary.csv) to verify zero mixed logs across retries.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-30","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"31. You discover a DDR bug one week before tape-out. What do you do?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nOne week before tape-out (or A0→B0 respin), engineering time is measured in hours. Escalating an unverified hunch wastes design cycles; sitting on a real bug until after tape-out costs millions in mask respins. The fastest way to help design/DFT teams is providing: (1) exact failing sequence/seed; (2) voltage/frequency/temperature sensitivity (does +50 mV or slower frequency fix it?); and (3) whether it's isolated to one channel/rank or systemic.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nOne week before tape-out (or A0→B0 respin), engineering time is measured in hours. Escalating an unverified hunch wastes design cycles; sitting on a real bug until after tape-out costs millions in mask respins. The fastest way to help design/DFT teams is providing: (1) exact failing sequence/seed; (2) voltage/frequency/temperature sensitivity (does +50 mV or slower frequency fix it?); and (3) whether it's isolated to one channel/rank or systemic.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Confirm reproduction 3+ times with SHA256-verified RTL/bitstream/FW and clean baseline logs.\n2. Run quick V/f shmoo and single-channel vs multi-channel check to bound the root cause.\n3. Package exact failing command, register dump (e.g., ZQCal, ISR, training status), and waveform/log excerpt into a 1-page escalation brief immediately.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-31","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"32. A board from another team shows inconsistent behavior. How do you handle the handoff?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nCross-team 'ghost bugs' frequently come from hidden environment differences: a different power supply current limit, a missing jumper/strap, an older A0 vs B0 firmware image, or mechanical damage during transport. Reproducing the sender's exact condition first—and keeping separate log files for baseline vs post-inspection—ensures you never confuse a bench mismatch with a silicon defect.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCross-team 'ghost bugs' frequently come from hidden environment differences: a different power supply current limit, a missing jumper/strap, an older A0 vs B0 firmware image, or mechanical damage during transport. Reproducing the sender's exact condition first—and keeping separate log files for baseline vs post-inspection—ensures you never confuse a bench mismatch with a silicon defect.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Document serial numbers, BOM/rework tags, jumper positions, and sha256sum of every firmware/script file handed off.\n2. Unpowered microscope check: inspect socket pins, BGA/rework areas, sense resistors, and connectors.\n3. Run the sender's exact command on their exact config first to establish baseline reproduction rate before A/B swapping.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-32","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"33. You're asked to validate 50 boards under thermal stress in 2 days. How do you plan and scale?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nWith 50 boards in 48 hours, manual clicking or sequential debugging will fail the schedule. You have to optimize throughput = (parallel stations × uptime) / cycle_time: prioritize the highest-risk PVT corners (hot soak + cold soak) over redundant intermediate steps, stage spare cables/adapters/fixtures so one worn contactor doesn't stall a rig, and automate log parsing so pass/fail decisions take seconds instead of manual spreadsheet review.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nWith 50 boards in 48 hours, manual clicking or sequential debugging will fail the schedule. You have to optimize throughput = (parallel stations × uptime) / cycle_time: prioritize the highest-risk PVT corners (hot soak + cold soak) over redundant intermediate steps, stage spare cables/adapters/fixtures so one worn contactor doesn't stall a rig, and automate log parsing so pass/fail decisions take seconds instead of manual spreadsheet review.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n2. Verify station-to-station correlation with one golden reference board across all chambers/heads before loading the 50-board lot.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-33","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"34. Tell me about a time you had to debug a critical issue under pressure.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nOne documented DRAM automation issue was a mismatch between a report and its underlying log. The report said no fail directories were needed, while iter_170/ap.log recorded zero passes and two failures. The captured rerun changed the reporting output to indicate mirrored fail directories; that supports a reporting-behavior improvement, not a claim that the DRAM fault was repaired.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nThe cold/PF13/ZQ observations are a separate investigation. Confirm timestamps, sensor identity, register semantics and a controlled reproduction before making a causal claim.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Preserve raw logs (AP_LOG, chamber CSV, dmesg) and freeze hardware state before touching anything.\n3. Reconstruct exact failing timeline (1m22s Loop 1 -> 1m27s 0x400/0x100 interrupts) and share a concise 1-page evidence packet.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-35","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"36. What's one lesson you learned from a failed board bring-up?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nChecking power rails, power sequencing, reference clocks, and reset release on the schematic before reflashing firmware saves hours—and keeping a clean baseline log before reseating or reworking prevents losing the original failure signature.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nFirmware is completely passive until hardware satisfies power, clock, and reset. Reflashing five BIOS builds onto a board whose TI UCD sequencer hasn't released PWRGOOD only adds confusion and risks damaging the socket or SPI flash pads. Turning every failed bring-up into a repeatable checklist (SOP/BKM) protects the whole lab.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify power rails -> clocks -> reset -> JTAG/SPI activity in strict order before reflashing.\n2. Change only one variable per retest and save separate logs for each attempt.\n3. Document the root cause and fix in the team's BKM/SOP.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-36","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"37. Sketch a block diagram showing how DAQ, Oscilloscope, and DUT connect for validation.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nSeparating the Oscilloscope (high-BW AC/transient capture) from the DAQ (high-resolution 24-hour multi-rail DC drift) while synchronizing both under one Host Control PC gives complete electrical observability without overloading one instrument. Proper ground isolation (differential DAQ channels, short scope ground springs, isolated bench supplies) prevents ground-loop noise or accidental rail shorts through earth ground.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nSeparating the Oscilloscope (high-BW AC/transient capture) from the DAQ (high-resolution 24-hour multi-rail DC drift) while synchronizing both under one Host Control PC gives complete electrical observability without overloading one instrument. Proper ground isolation (differential DAQ channels, short scope ground springs, isolated bench supplies) prevents ground-loop noise or accidental rail shorts through earth ground.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify common ground safety and differential DAQ common-mode limits before powering the DUT.\n3. Verify scope trigger sync (e.g., triggering on current step or GPIO sync pulse) aligns with DUT workload start.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-37","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"38. Write pseudocode for automating USB interface validation across 5 test cases.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA USB 3.0 port can silently drop to USB 2.0 (480 Mbps) or rack up xHCI transfer retries while a simple file copy still technically finishes. Validating both negotiated SuperSpeed link rate and zero kernel/CRC errors catches marginal signal integrity, bad AC coupling caps, or retimer bugs that functional-only scripts miss.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA USB 3.0 port can silently drop to USB 2.0 (480 Mbps) or rack up xHCI transfer retries while a simple file copy still technically finishes. Validating both negotiated SuperSpeed link rate and zero kernel/CRC errors catches marginal signal integrity, bad AC coupling caps, or retimer bugs that functional-only scripts miss.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify lsusb -t reports 5000M or 10000M (not 480M) before and after every suspend/reset test.\n2. Check dmesg -T | grep -iE 'usb|xhci' after every iteration for silent link resets or descriptor read retries.\n3. Verify data integrity with sha256sum on read-back files, not just throughput MB/s.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.0-38","topic":"POST SILICON QA","question":"39. Design a test matrix to validate DDR stability under voltage and frequency scaling.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nTesting only steady-state corners misses transition droop—when memtester dvfs steps between PF states (like PF0 to PF13 or PF16), VDD_GMC and VDDQ_GMC experience instantaneous load changes; if the PMIC can't settle before the next memory transaction, pointers corrupt or ZQCal drifts. Combining steady-state holds with dynamic PF transitions across hot (leakage/setup) and cold (ZQCal saturation/drive strength) corners gives complete coverage.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nTesting only steady-state corners misses transition droop—when memtester dvfs steps between PF states (like PF0 to PF13 or PF16), VDD_GMC and VDDQ_GMC experience instantaneous load changes; if the PMIC can't settle before the next memory transaction, pointers corrupt or ZQCal drifts. Combining steady-state holds with dynamic PF transitions across hot (leakage/setup) and cold (ZQCal saturation/drive strength) corners gives complete coverage.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify thermal head T-Case soak stability (±1 °C) at each temperature corner before starting the matrix.\n2. Run steady-state holds at each PF state first, then dynamic PF-transition sweeps (pattern [0,8,10,16]).\n4. Compare vector Vmin against fused-voltage 10% screening margin and export SMV1D/SMV2D heatmaps in JMP/JSL.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-1","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 1 Follow-up Q2: Which project are you most proud of and why?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIn automated 24-hour thermal/Vmin testing, dual-head controllers can suffer command collisions or begin stressing a DUT before die T-Case actually stabilizes, creating false silicon failures. Head-level locking, acknowledgement parsing and temperature checks are controls intended to improve repeatability; their effect requires before/after run evidence.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn automated 24-hour thermal/Vmin testing, dual-head controllers can suffer command collisions or begin stressing a DUT before die T-Case actually stabilizes, creating false silicon failures. Head-level locking, acknowledgement parsing and temperature checks are controls intended to improve repeatability; their effect requires before/after run evidence.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify head-level lock release inside try/finally and separately test controller watchdogs and safe-state behavior after host crash or communication loss.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-2","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 2 (ASIC Validation) Q3: How do you differentiate between ATE testing and System Level Testing (SLT)?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nSystem Level Testing puts that packaged SoC into a real motherboard or platform booting actual firmware and Linux, running concurrent CPU, GPU, PCIe, and DRAM workloads over minutes or hours to catch functional, thermal, and multi-domain timing escapes that ATE patterns cannot reproduce.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nATE tests blocks largely in isolation under controlled tester power supplies and rigid vector timing. Real systems introduce: (1) simultaneous switching noise across multiple domains (CPU + GPU + NoC + DRAM) causing first-droop IR collapse; (2) dynamic DVFS/PF-state transitions and real PMIC settling loops; (3) full protocol state machines (PCIe Gen4/5 LTSSM equalization, DDR MRC 2D training); and (4) self-heating thermal gradients. Because SLT takes minutes per unit while ATE takes seconds, SLT's mission is both final quality gating and feeding escape signatures back to DFT/ATE to tighten structural screens.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Compare ATE bin/Vmin logs against SLT failing PF state, temperature, and workload to see if the escape is speed-path, PDN-droop, or protocol-specific.\n2. Check if raising VDD by +25–50 mV on the SLT bench makes the ATE-pass chip pass SLT—showing voltage sensitivity without uniquely identifying a timing or IR-drop cause.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-3","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 2 Follow-up Q4: If a chip passes ATE but fails SLT, what would be your first debug step?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA huge percentage of 'ATE-pass / SLT-fail' units in real labs turn out to be worn socket contactors, bent pins, wrong firmware stepping (A0 vs B0), or unstabilized T-Case soak. Once A/B swapping proves the failure follows the silicon, you characterize the failing boundary (voltage shmoo, frequency shmoo, temperature sensitivity, and failing IP block like DDR vs PCIe) to give ASIC/DFT teams an exact reproduction recipe.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA huge percentage of 'ATE-pass / SLT-fail' units in real labs turn out to be worn socket contactors, bent pins, wrong firmware stepping (A0 vs B0), or unstabilized T-Case soak. Once A/B swapping proves the failure follows the silicon, you characterize the failing boundary (voltage shmoo, frequency shmoo, temperature sensitivity, and failing IP block like DDR vs PCIe) to give ASIC/DFT teams an exact reproduction recipe.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Preserve failing UART/dmesg/AER/thermal logs and firmware sha256sum before removing the chip.\n2. Inspect socket contacts under a microscope and reseat once to test repeatability.\n3. Execute A/B cross-swap with a golden reference DUT on a verified reference board.\n4. On confirmed silicon fails, sweep VDD (+/−50 mV) and lock DVFS/PCIe Gen speed to isolate timing vs functional defect.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-4","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 3 (DFT Engineer) Q5: How familiar are you with scan chains or BIST structures in post-silicon validation?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nScan chains stitch internal flip-flops into serial shift registers so ATPG patterns can shift in test states and shift out captured responses, while MBIST and LBIST are on-die self-test engines that test dense SRAM arrays and logic at-speed without external pin bottlenecks.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nScan solves internal controllability and observability for stuck-at and transition-delay faults, while MBIST runs deterministic algorithmic sequences (like March C-, checkerboard, address-complement) across every SRAM bank, row, and column—often feeding redundancy/repair fuses. In post-silicon SLT, DFT hooks remain invaluable: scan-dumps capture internal architectural state after a system hang, and BIST/PRBS engines isolate failing SRAM banks or SerDes lanes.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Check if the failing SLT subsystem exposes on-die MBIST status/repair registers or SerDes PRBS/loopback counters.\n2. On a silent hang, coordinate with DFT to trigger JTAG scan-dump / crash-dump before cycling board power.\n3. Compare failing SLT bit/lane addresses against ATE scan/MBIST datalog bins for that die ECID.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-5","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 3 Follow-up Q6: How would you leverage DFT to debug a failing subsystem?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nFunctional OS workloads exercise millions of transistors at once, making root-cause localization noisy. DFT modes strip away OS/driver/NoC variables: if SerDes PRBS-31 passes in internal digital loopback but fails in external pad loopback, the fault is strictly in the analog front-end, package ball, socket, or PCB trace rather than digital RTL. Similarly, an MBIST fail bitmap pinpoints exact failing row/column/bank.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nFunctional OS workloads exercise millions of transistors at once, making root-cause localization noisy. DFT modes strip away OS/driver/NoC variables: if SerDes PRBS-31 passes in internal digital loopback but fails in external pad loopback, the fault is strictly in the analog front-end, package ball, socket, or PCB trace rather than digital RTL. Similarly, an MBIST fail bitmap pinpoints exact failing row/column/bank.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Read chip ECID/fuse ID so DFT can pull wafer/ATE wafer-sort history.\n2. Run internal digital loopback vs external pad/fixture loopback (or MBIST at failing voltage/temp corner).\n3. Sweep VDD ±50 mV during the DFT hook to determine whether it is a hard physical defect or a Vmin timing margin wall.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-6","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 4 (Hardware Engineer) Q7: Suppose DDR initialization is failing intermittently. What debug methods would you apply?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIntermittent DDR init failures happen when a training margin sits right on the pass/fail cliff—so small shifts in contact resistance, VDDQ_GMC ripple, temperature, or ZQCal (PCODE/NCODE) push Status 7 (pass) into Status ff (fail). Keeping cold boot, warm reset, and sleep-resume separate also reveals whether stale controller state is leaking across resets.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIntermittent DDR init failures happen when a training margin sits right on the pass/fail cliff—so small shifts in contact resistance, VDDQ_GMC ripple, temperature, or ZQCal (PCODE/NCODE) push Status 7 (pass) into Status ff (fail). Keeping cold boot, warm reset, and sleep-resume separate also reveals whether stale controller state is leaking across resets.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n3. Probe VDD_GMC and VDDQ_GMC during init with AC-coupled scope (10–50 mV/div, < 5 mm ground spring) for droop or phase noise.\n4. Inspect socket/contactor under microscope, reseat original assembly, repeat ≥ 10 cold boots, then A/B swap with golden reference.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-7","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 4 Follow-up Q8: How do you verify signal integrity and timing margins?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA functional pass records success for the tested workload and conditions; it does not quantify positive timing or voltage margin—it cannot predict yield across manufacturing skew.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA functional pass records success for the tested workload and conditions; it does not quantify positive timing or voltage margin—it cannot predict yield across manufacturing skew.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Scope SI/PI check: measure Vpp, V_min undershoot, V_max overshoot, settling time, and clock excursion under worst-case dI/dt stress.\n2. PHY eye check: run SMV1D/SMV2D shmoo across byte lanes and flag any asymmetrical or narrowed lane (check ZQCal PU/PD spread).\n3. PVT check: compare room (25 °C) vs hot (85 °C+) and cold (−10 °C) eye plots and Vmin against the 10% screening margin.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-8","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 5 (Software/Automation Lead) Q9: Write a Python snippet that searches log files for critical errors.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nStreaming lines limits input buffering, but this example accumulates findings and therefore uses memory proportional to the number of matches, allowing a single script to scan 10 GB dmesg/UART logs on a lab host without OOM kills. Compiling re.compile(...) avoids per-line regex compilation overhead, and word boundaries (\\b) reduce false positives. CODE EXAMPLE · Validate instrument commands and failure handling before bench use: import re from pathlib import Path CRITICAL_RE = re.compile(r'\\b(CRITICAL|FATAL|PANIC|ERROR|FAIL|ECC|AER|Xid)\\b', re.IGNORECASE) def find_critical_errors(log_path: str\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nStreaming lines limits input buffering, but this example accumulates findings and therefore uses memory proportional to the number of matches, allowing a single script to scan 10 GB dmesg/UART logs on a lab host without OOM kills. Compiling re.compile(...) avoids per-line regex compilation overhead, and word boundaries (\\b) reduce false positives. CODE EXAMPLE · Validate instrument commands and failure handling before bench use: import re from pathlib import Path CRITICAL_RE = re.compile(r'\\b(CRITICAL|FATAL|PANIC|ERROR|FAIL|ECC|AER|Xid)\\b', re.IGNORECASE) def find_critical_errors(log_path: str) -> list[dict]: findings = [] with Path(log_path).open('r', encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as fh: for line_no, line in enumerate(fh, start=1): m = CRITICAL_RE.search(line) if m: findings.append({'line_no': line_no, 'keyword': m.group(1).upper()\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. This keyword matcher has no semantic pass/fail exclusions. Test zero-error summaries, nonzero fail counters, expected-error tests and incomplete runs; use a separate status parser before classifying a run.\n2. Test with binary/non-UTF8 bytes injected to verify errors='replace' handles corrupted UART streams gracefully.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-9","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 5 Follow-up Q10: How would you extend it to store results in CSV with timestamps?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nStreaming directly to csv.DictWriter keeps memory constant even if a failing test emits 500,000 error lines, while preserving log_timestamp lets engineers align an AER or MEMTESTER ERROR event to the exact second a thermal chamber crossed −25.56 °C or a VRM dipped.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nStreaming directly to csv.DictWriter keeps memory constant even if a failing test emits 500,000 error lines, while preserving log_timestamp lets engineers align an AER or MEMTESTER ERROR event to the exact second a thermal chamber crossed −25.56 °C or a VRM dipped.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify newline='' in open() so Windows/Excel/JMP doesn't see blank double-spaced rows.\n2. Verify CSV opens cleanly in pandas.read_csv() and JMP even when error messages contain commas or quotes.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-10","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 1 (Hiring Manager) Q11: We discovered a silicon bug during validation. Fixing requires a respin but schedule is tight. How do you decide?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA respin costs millions and months of schedule, while shipping an unmitigated silent-data-corruption (SDC) bug can trigger massive field recalls. Validating a proposed workaround requires proving it doesn't merely hide the failure by skipping the stressed PF state or blowing TDP/yield budgets—for example, raising Vmin by +50 mV might pass functionally but fail 10% fused-voltage screening or thermal spec.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA respin costs millions and months of schedule, while shipping an unmitigated silent-data-corruption (SDC) bug can trigger massive field recalls. Validating a proposed workaround requires proving it doesn't merely hide the failure by skipping the stressed PF state or blowing TDP/yield budgets—for example, raising Vmin by +50 mV might pass functionally but fail 10% fused-voltage screening or thermal spec.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Quantify exact failure rate across multiple units, skewed corners (SS/TT/FF), and full temperature range.\n2. Test any proposed FW/DVFS/MRC workaround across all PVT corners and check power/clock/yield impact.\n3. Present confirmed data (never speculation) in a Go/No-Go risk table.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-11","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 1 Follow-up Q12: How would you communicate this to cross-functional leadership?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nLeadership needs risk, yield %, and schedule options in 30 seconds; ASIC/DFT engineers need exact failing PF state, register addresses, and waveforms to act immediately. Separating confirmed observations from open hypotheses builds instant credibility across teams.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nLeadership needs risk, yield %, and schedule options in 30 seconds; ASIC/DFT engineers need exact failing PF state, register addresses, and waveforms to act immediately. Separating confirmed observations from open hypotheses builds instant credibility across teams.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify every number, SKU, and SHA256 in the summary against raw logs before sending.\n2. Include clear owner + next experiment + ETA for any unconfirmed root cause.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-12","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 2 (Quality/Process) Q13: How do you ensure your test process continuously improves?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nContinuous improvement in SLT directly drives yield enhancement and test-time reduction. Lowering false-fail rates via socket preventive maintenance and automated pre-flight checks saves tester capacity and prevents good silicon from being mis-binned.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nContinuous improvement in SLT directly drives yield enhancement and test-time reduction. Lowering false-fail rates via socket preventive maintenance and automated pre-flight checks saves tester capacity and prevents good silicon from being mis-binned.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Review weekly retest/false-fail Pareto by station/socket ID to catch degrading fixtures early.\n2. Audit SOP/BKM docs after every major debug so fixes become permanent team habits.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.63ux3dgztp8y-13","topic":"SYSTEM LEVEL QA","question":"Panelist 2 Follow-up Q14: Give an example of a workflow you optimized in your last role.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nBefore locking and ack checks, concurrent scripts could overwrite setpoints mid-soak. These automation features target repeatability and analysis effort. The supplied material does not establish that they eliminated overnight aborts or completed SKU sign-off.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nBefore locking and ack checks, concurrent scripts could overwrite setpoints mid-soak. These automation features target repeatability and analysis effort. The supplied material does not establish that they eliminated overnight aborts or completed SKU sign-off.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify lock acquisition/release logs and T-Case soak timestamps in thermal CSVs.\n2. Spot-check JMP/JSL heatmap outputs against raw SMV2D CSV rows to guarantee parser accuracy.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.842ee7x0mlgm-0","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Q1: Read voltage from a DMM using pyvisa and log to CSV","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nInstrument handles left open after an exception lock out subsequent test runs on shared lab benches, and OS write buffering can leave a 0-byte CSV if a host reboots mid-soak. Using try/finally + f.flush() makes the logger safe for 24-hour runs.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nInstrument handles left open after an exception lock out subsequent test runs on shared lab benches, and OS write buffering can leave a 0-byte CSV if a host reboots mid-soak. Using try/finally + f.flush() makes the logger safe for 24-hour runs.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run rm.list_resources() and dmm.query('*IDN?') first to verify VISA backend and USB/LAN address.\n2. Verify DMM range/NPLC integration time isn't slower than interval_s.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.842ee7x0mlgm-1","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Q2: Live temperature plotting using matplotlib","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIn long thermal soak tests, unbounded lists and repeated ax.clear() calls bog down the UI thread. On headless Linux SLT servers without X11, switching to matplotlib.use('Agg') and saving periodic PNG snapshots avoids display crashes.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn long thermal soak tests, unbounded lists and repeated ax.clear() calls bog down the UI thread. On headless Linux SLT servers without X11, switching to matplotlib.use('Agg') and saving periodic PNG snapshots avoids display crashes.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify DISPLAY or use Agg backend if running over headless SSH.\n2. Bound deque(maxlen=...) so 24-hour runs keep constant RAM.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.842ee7x0mlgm-2","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Q3: Basic test framework structure (Setup, Run, Collect, Cleanup)","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIn automated thermal/Vmin benches, an unhandled TimeoutError without finally cleanup leaves a Chroma Cobra head at −25 °C or +105 °C and leaves lock files behind, blocking the next test. CODE EXAMPLE · Validate instrument commands and failure handling before bench use: import logging def run_slt_suite(dut, thermal, instruments) -> bool: try: logging.info('1. Setup: verifying serial, SSH, and thermal head lock') instruments.connect(); thermal.set_and_wait(85.0) logging.info('2. Run: executing DVFS + memtester + PCIe stress') passed = dut.run_stress() logging.info('3. Collect: pulling dmesg, UAR\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn automated thermal/Vmin benches, an unhandled TimeoutError without finally cleanup leaves a Chroma Cobra head at −25 °C or +105 °C and leaves lock files behind, blocking the next test. CODE EXAMPLE · Validate instrument commands and failure handling before bench use: import logging def run_slt_suite(dut, thermal, instruments) -> bool: try: logging.info('1. Setup: verifying serial, SSH, and thermal head lock') instruments.connect(); thermal.set_and_wait(85.0) logging.info('2. Run: executing DVFS + memtester + PCIe stress') passed = dut.run_stress() logging.info('3. Collect: pulling dmesg, UART, and scope captures') dut.collect_logs() return passed finally: logging.info('4. Cleanup: returning thermal head to 25C & releasing locks') thermal.safe_idle(25.0); instruments.close()\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inject a simulated TimeoutError during Run in a unit test and verify thermal.safe_idle(25.0) still executes.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.842ee7x0mlgm-8","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Question1: How would you read a large file line by line in Python without consuming too much memory?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIterate directly over the open file handle (for line in f:) or wrap it in a generator (yield), which streams the file in constant O(1) memory instead of loading gigabytes into RAM like f.read() or f.readlines().\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nFor .gz compressed SLT archives, gzip.open(path, 'rt', errors='replace') streams line-by-line without decompressing to disk.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Check peak RSS with tracemalloc or /usr/bin/time -v on a > 1 GB test log to confirm flat memory usage.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.842ee7x0mlgm-9","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Question2: Explain what a lambda function is in Python with a validation use case.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA lambda is a concise, single-expression anonymous function (lambda args: expr), ideal as a short sort key or filter predicate—for instance sorting PCIe lanes by error count: sorted(lanes, key=lambda r: r['aer_errors'], reverse=True).\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nLambdas cannot contain statements or multi-line blocks; anything requiring error handling, logging, or reuse should be a named def function.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify the sort key handles missing/None values cleanly (e.g., r.get('aer_errors', 0)).","source":"#"},{"id":"t.842ee7x0mlgm-10","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Question3: Describe the difference between deepcopy and shallow copy in Python.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\ncopy.copy() duplicates only the outer container while sharing references to nested mutable objects, whereas copy.deepcopy() recursively clones every nested list or dictionary. In SLT bench configs, deepcopy prevents one DUT's voltage override from accidentally mutating the shared baseline template.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIf base_cfg = {'dvfs': {'v_mv': 750}}, doing dut2 = base_cfg.copy() and setting dut2['dvfs']['v_mv'] = 700 silently overwrites base_cfg['dvfs']['v_mv'] too!\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Assert id(dut2['dvfs']) != id(base_cfg['dvfs']) in config-loader unit tests.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.842ee7x0mlgm-11","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Question4: How do you handle exceptions in Python automation scripts?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nUsing bare except: pass hides real bugs and turns broken runs into false PASSes; catching specific transport errors lets you retry cleanly while letting KeyboardInterrupt or ValueError surface immediately.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nUsing bare except: pass hides real bugs and turns broken runs into false PASSes; catching specific transport errors lets you retry cleanly while letting KeyboardInterrupt or ValueError surface immediately.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify finally cleanup runs cleanly on both TimeoutError and Ctrl+C (KeyboardInterrupt).","source":"#"},{"id":"t.842ee7x0mlgm-13","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Question6: How would you optimize a Python program for multithreading vs multiprocessing?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nThreads share memory (so shared hardware controllers like a dual-head Chroma Cobra need a threading.Lock()), whereas processes have separate memory spaces and true multi-core parallelism.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nThreads share memory (so shared hardware controllers like a dual-head Chroma Cobra need a threading.Lock()), whereas processes have separate memory spaces and true multi-core parallelism.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Protect shared serial/TCP thermal controllers with a mutex lock; benchmark single-thread vs pool runtime.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.hxhq6pol1183-0","topic":"Assembly of Socket for Hardware (From Scratch) QA","question":"1. How do you prepare the workspace and identify the socket before assembly?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nPre-production silicon and fine-pitch sockets are vulnerable to latent ESD damage and mechanical warpage if the PCB isn't supported flat on a rigid jig during placement and reflow.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nPre-production silicon and fine-pitch sockets are vulnerable to latent ESD damage and mechanical warpage if the PCB isn't supported flat on a rigid jig during placement and reflow.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Test wrist strap & ESD mat ground continuity.\n2. Verify socket part number, pitch, and PCB footprint revision match the BOM.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.hxhq6pol1183-1","topic":"Assembly of Socket for Hardware (From Scratch) QA","question":"2. How do you prepare PCB pads and apply solder paste for BGA/LGA sockets?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nExcess paste causes hidden solder bridges underneath the socket body, while insufficient paste causes cold/open joints—both of which create intermittent high-speed lane or power-delivery failures.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nExcess paste causes hidden solder bridges underneath the socket body, while insufficient paste causes cold/open joints—both of which create intermittent high-speed lane or power-delivery failures.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inspect stenciled paste bricks under the microscope for smearing or bridged apertures before placing the socket.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.hxhq6pol1183-6","topic":"Assembly of Socket for Hardware (From Scratch) QA","question":"7. How do you isolate a bad socket/contactor from a bad silicon chip in SLT?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIf multiple unrelated chips fail the same pin/lane/channel inside one socket while passing in another station, the fault is fixture wear rather than silicon.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIf multiple unrelated chips fail the same pin/lane/channel inside one socket while passing in another station, the fault is fixture wear rather than silicon.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Inspect contactors under high magnification and log insertion-cycle count.\n2. Run A/B cross-swap with a golden chip before binning any DUT as a silicon failure.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.bmh0yfwnrbk-1","topic":"Linux Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Linux Q2: How do you verify the Linux OS, kernel, and system resource baseline before running GPU/SLT diagnostics?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA silent kernel update (uname -r mismatch) can leave the driver unbuilt for the active kernel. Similarly, a full root filesystem (df -h at 100%) prevents log files, core dumps, and driver persistence sockets from being written, causing false test failures. Checking uptime also reveals if the host silently rebooted mid-overnight run.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA silent kernel update (uname -r mismatch) can leave the driver unbuilt for the active kernel. Similarly, a full root filesystem (df -h at 100%) prevents log files, core dumps, and driver persistence sockets from being written, causing false test failures. Checking uptime also reveals if the host silently rebooted mid-overnight run.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run uname -a and lsb_release -a and compare against the approved SLT test-station OS/kernel manifest.\n2. Run df -h and free -h to confirm /var/log and /tmp have free space and host DRAM isn't OOM-thrashing.\n3. Check uptime and who -b to verify the machine didn't reset unexpectedly before or during the run.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.bmh0yfwnrbk-4","topic":"Linux Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Linux Q5: How do you inspect kernel and system logs when a GPU drops off the bus, a driver crashes, or the system fails to boot?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nBecause dmesg resets on reboot, journalctl -b -1 (when persistent journald storage is enabled) is essential for post-mortem analysis after a hard lockup or power loss.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nBecause dmesg resets on reboot, journalctl -b -1 (when persistent journald storage is enabled) is essential for post-mortem analysis after a hard lockup or power loss.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n2. Check journalctl -b -1 -p err if the host crashed or rebooted during the previous test run.\n3. Export both logs cleanly (dmesg -T > dmesg.log and journalctl -xe > journal.log) before power-cycling.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.bmh0yfwnrbk-6","topic":"Linux Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Linux Q7: How do you monitor system thermals, fans, and power supplies on a Linux test station?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nThermal throttling happens when junction or board temperatures exceed firmware thresholds, causing clocks and voltage to step down to protect silicon—which can masquerade as a performance regression or intermittent timing failure.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nThermal throttling happens when junction or board temperatures exceed firmware thresholds, causing clocks and voltage to step down to protect silicon—which can masquerade as a performance regression or intermittent timing failure.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run sensors to verify CPU, ambient, and fan RPMs are within expected bench limits.\n3. Log watch -n 1 or CSV sensor polling alongside the workload so thermal ramps are timestamped.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.bmh0yfwnrbk-7","topic":"Linux Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Linux Q8: How do you check USB debug adapters, block storage, NVMe drives, and network interfaces on a Linux bench host?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIn an SLT or lab rack, a 'hung DUT' or 'failed log transfer' often traces back to a flaky USB-UART bridge, a dropped 10GbE/1GbE management NIC (ethtool showing Link detected: no or negotiated down to 100Mb/s), or a full/read-only NVMe scratch drive.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn an SLT or lab rack, a 'hung DUT' or 'failed log transfer' often traces back to a flaky USB-UART bridge, a dropped 10GbE/1GbE management NIC (ethtool showing Link detected: no or negotiated down to 100Mb/s), or a full/read-only NVMe scratch drive.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n2. Run ip link and sudo ethtool <iface> to confirm physical carrier, speed, and duplex on management/PXE NICs.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.9z8bwhz8vf5g-1","topic":"High-Level Debug Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"HLD Q2 (Steps 2–3): The target log stopped mid-run. How do you distinguish a Linux USB-UART drop from a hung DUT?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nEqually important, you should never launch a second serial terminal on a port already owned by the logger, because competing readers steal bytes and corrupt log parsing. And fixing a USB cable does not justify swapping boards; you retest the exact original assembly first.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nEqually important, you should never launch a second serial terminal on a port already owned by the logger, because competing readers steal bytes and corrupt log parsing. And fixing a USB cable does not justify swapping boards; you retest the exact original assembly first.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n2. Check lsof \"$AP_PORT\" to verify no competing process is reading the UART port.\n3. After replacing a suspect cable/hub, re-run the exact diagnostic on the original board and classify the earlier incomplete log as invalid.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.9z8bwhz8vf5g-2","topic":"High-Level Debug Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"HLD Q3 (Step 4): How do you rule out a firmware or image mismatch before blaming the silicon or socket?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nA SHA-256 match only proves the file wasn't corrupted on disk—it does not prove it is the right binary for that SKU or silicon stepping. For instance, loading an A0-compiled DRAM init binary onto B0 silicon produces initialization/training failures that look electrical. Also, a known-good reference unit with a different DRAM SKU must use its own matching supported firmware rather than forcing identical filenames.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA SHA-256 match only proves the file wasn't corrupted on disk—it does not prove it is the right binary for that SKU or silicon stepping. For instance, loading an A0-compiled DRAM init binary onto B0 silicon produces initialization/training failures that look electrical. Also, a known-good reference unit with a different DRAM SKU must use its own matching supported firmware rather than forcing identical filenames.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Run sha256sum -c ./SHA256SUMS on the host provisioning directory.\n2. Compare the target's boot/BL1/BL2 banner (chip rev, DRAM vendor/capacity) against the firmware release manifest.\n3. If a mismatch is found, document it, flash the correct package, and establish a fresh baseline on the original assembly.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.9z8bwhz8vf5g-3","topic":"High-Level Debug Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"HLD Q4 (Steps 5–6): When is it safe to power-cycle a failing assembly, and how do you structure the restart?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nOnly after removing power and visually inspecting power rails, UART wiring, socket alignment, basket seating, retention hardware, and pins for contamination or damage.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCold power-on, warm reset, and sleep-resume leave completely different amounts of residual state inside the memory controller, PHY PLLs, and DRAM mode registers, so they must never be conflated. Likewise, combining cleaning, reseating, firmware changes, and voltage margining in a single step makes it impossible to know which action actually cured the symptom.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Power off and inspect socket pins, basket alignment, retention torque/latch, and power/UART headers.\n2. Perform a clean cold power-cycle while recording UART from initial boot (BL1/BL2/dram_init).\n3. Record whether the failure reproduces deterministically from cold boot or depends on prior state.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.9z8bwhz8vf5g-4","topic":"High-Level Debug Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"HLD Q5 (Step 7): If socket seating is suspected, how do you test that hypothesis cleanly without fooling yourself?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nThere is no Linux command that proves every DRAM ball or socket pin is making low-resistance contact; lsusb and readlink only confirm the host UART interface is back. Real proof of seating recovery comes from repeating initialization, ZQ calibration values, functional memtester loops, and electrical eye/margin measurements across multiple runs.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nThere is no Linux command that proves every DRAM ball or socket pin is making low-resistance contact; lsusb and readlink only confirm the host UART interface is back. Real proof of seating recovery comes from repeating initialization, ZQ calibration values, functional memtester loops, and electrical eye/margin measurements across multiple runs.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n2. Compare ZQ calibration codes and training eye margins before vs. after reseat on the exact same unit.\n3. Run multiple consecutive loops/reboots before attributing recovery to seating.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.9z8bwhz8vf5g-5","topic":"High-Level Debug Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"HLD Q6 (Steps 8–9): Walk me through how you use a known-good reference unit and A-B-A swaps to isolate DRAM vs. board vs. socket.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nControlled A-B-A substitution isolates whether a defect follows the DUT or stays with the station. If suspect DRAM fails on both setups while reference DRAM passes on both, the fault follows the DRAM assembly. If both fail only on the original fixture, the shared power/routing/socket path is guilty. Note that if SoC and DRAM are co-packaged or move together as one assembly, swapping that assembly isolates the combined package—not the DRAM die independently.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nControlled A-B-A substitution isolates whether a defect follows the DUT or stays with the station. If suspect DRAM fails on both setups while reference DRAM passes on both, the fault follows the DRAM assembly. If both fail only on the original fixture, the shared power/routing/socket path is guilty. Note that if SoC and DRAM are co-packaged or move together as one assembly, swapping that assembly isolates the combined package—not the DRAM die independently.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify reference unit baseline pass on its native known-good bench first.\n2. Cross-test: suspect DRAM -> known-good board, and known-good DRAM -> suspect board (matching SKU firmware).\n3. After any fixture repair, always reinstall and retest the original failing assembly to close the loop.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.9z8bwhz8vf5g-6","topic":"High-Level Debug Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"HLD Q7 (Step 10): How do you separate a firmware regression from a parser/automation bug when a test starts failing?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nEven when a newer firmware change makes a failing test pass, you cannot sign it off blindly—it might have fixed a timing table or calibration sequence, or it might have silently skipped a failing PF state or relaxed a margin! You must verify that the exact same operating states, frequencies, voltages, and margins were actually exercised.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nEven when a newer firmware change makes a failing test pass, you cannot sign it off blindly—it might have fixed a timing table or calibration sequence, or it might have silently skipped a failing PF state or relaxed a margin! You must verify that the exact same operating states, frequencies, voltages, and margins were actually exercised.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Diff the raw UART log against the automation pass/fail parser output to rule out script bugs.\n2. A/B test baseline FW vs. suspect FW on identical hardware/temperature.\n3. Compare actual exercised PF states, clock frequencies, and shmoo margins between passing and failing FW.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.9z8bwhz8vf5g-7","topic":"High-Level Debug Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"HLD Q8 (Steps 11–12): In memtester dvfs pattern [0,8,10,16] loops 50 size 16 MB, what does each parameter tell you during debug?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nmemtester is the target memory diagnostic; dvfs exercises dynamic voltage and frequency scaling states; pattern [0,8,10,16] selects specific data stimulus IDs defined by the firmware; loops 50 sets the repetition count; and size 16 MB requests a test size; actual allocation, address coverage, cache behavior and rank mapping require confirmation in the matching firmware.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA common trap is guessing what 0,8,10,16 mean without checking the firmware's pattern table—they are stimulus pattern IDs, not voltage states. Isolating which pattern fails guides physical debug: checkerboard exposes adjacent-line crosstalk/coupling, solid 1s/0s expose DC power droop or stuck faults, and address patterns expose decode/aliasing issues. Likewise, you must verify actual rank/cache mapping before claiming full DRAM coverage.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Keep separate log files per run and follow live with tail -F \"$AP_LOG\".\n2. Run grep -nE 'Loop count number|Failed at PF State|MEMTESTER ERROR|Pass count|Fail count|ZQCal' \"$AP_LOG\".\n3. Look up the exact firmware pattern-ID table for [0,8,10,16] and check buffer allocation across Rank 0 / Rank 1.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.9z8bwhz8vf5g-8","topic":"High-Level Debug Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"HLD Q9 (Steps 13–14): Reconstruct a failing memtester loop—how do you interpret PF13 reports, unwanted interrupts, and sensor vs. chamber temperatures?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nSeparating fixed-PF13 operation from DVFS transitions tells you whether voltage settling/clock-switch sequencing is failing versus steady-state electrical margin. And because chamber air (-25.6C) and on-die sensors (-8C/-3C) sit at different physical locations, appended chamber readings cannot be blindly treated as die temperature.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nSeparating fixed-PF13 operation from DVFS transitions tells you whether voltage settling/clock-switch sequencing is failing versus steady-state electrical margin. And because chamber air (-25.6C) and on-die sensors (-8C/-3C) sit at different physical locations, appended chamber readings cannot be blindly treated as die temperature.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Check timestamps of Loop 1 start (1m22s) through exit (1m27s) to verify actual executed loop count.\n2. Compare fixed-state PF13 execution against dynamic DVFS transitions into/out of PF13.\n3. Correlate memss/aoc on-die sensors with chamber thermocouple timestamps using verified run boundaries.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.8avzqrpfy680-1","topic":"Communication Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Comm Q2: Tell me about a time your attention to detail caught a subtle hardware or test issue.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nSeparating those facts prevented a false root-cause conclusion and focused debug on the exact PF13 interrupt and ZQCal state.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIn SLT, the biggest time sink is chasing the wrong root cause because someone read a summary pass/fail line instead of the raw log and timestamps. Checking register bit positions, exact timestamps, and sensor locations before declaring a defect is the hallmark of a reliable validation engineer.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. State the exact discrepancy you noticed (summary vs. raw log timestamps/sensors).\n2. Explain the verification check you ran to confirm it wasn't a guess.\n3. Highlight the concrete outcome: preserved evidence and a more precise investigation; do not invent a time saving or avoided-swap count.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.8avzqrpfy680-2","topic":"Communication Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Comm Q3: Describe how you work in a team and communicate with design, firmware, or senior engineers when escalating a bug.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nThat respects everyone's time and builds trust across lab technicians, firmware engineers, and silicon architects.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCross-functional SLT teams move fast under tape-out and production schedules. When a technician or engineer escalates cleanly—separating observed facts from hypotheses—designers can act in minutes instead of asking five rounds of follow-up questions.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Confirm the bug is reproducible (or document exact intermittent rate, e.g., 2/39 runs).\n2. Bundle logs, HW/FW versions, and scope waveforms into one clean ticket.\n3. Clearly separate observed telemetry from your working hypothesis.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.8avzqrpfy680-3","topic":"Communication Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Comm Q4: How do you handle shifting priorities, unexpected hardware bottlenecks, or changing test plans (Flexibility)?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIn bring-up and SLT labs, priorities change whenever a new silicon stepping arrives or a hot customer-blocking bug appears.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nLab teams need engineers who don't get rattled when a chamber goes down, a fixture breaks, or a priority shift happens mid-day. Staying methodical under pressure protects expensive silicon and prevents half-finished experiments from polluting test data.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Checkpoint current logs and label hardware state before pivoting tasks.\n2. Clarify scope, deadline, and pass/fail criteria for the new urgent task.\n3. Share a concise handoff note if another teammate picks up the paused bench.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.puny9iguska8-0","topic":"Fundamental Knowledge Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Fund Q1: What is the fundamental relationship between charge, capacitance, and voltage in a DRAM cell?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nCharge equals capacitance times voltage (Q = C × V), where Q is in coulombs, C is in farads, and V is in volts. A 1T1C DRAM cell stores a bit as charge on a tiny capacitor. When the wordline turns on the access transistor during a read, that stored charge shares onto a much larger precharged bitline, creating a small voltage shift that the sense amplifier detects and restores.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nBecause DRAM storage cells are microscopic (tens of femtofarads), charge Q leaks over milliseconds through junction and subthreshold leakage—which is why DRAM requires periodic refresh (tREFI) and why reads are destructive until the sense amplifier drives the bitline back to full rail. Any drop in V_cell from leakage or insufficient write-recovery time directly shrinks the charge available for sensing.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. If bit-flips disappear when refresh interval (tREFI) is shortened or temperature is lowered, suspect retention/leakage decay.\n2. Check tWR (write recovery) and supply voltage margins if cells fail right after writes.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.puny9iguska8-1","topic":"Fundamental Knowledge Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Fund Q2: How do you calculate the bitline voltage change ΔV_BL caused by DRAM cell and bitline charge sharing?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nYou compute it in three steps: first find the capacitance ratio k between the cell and the total cell-plus-bitline capacitance, second find the initial voltage difference between V_cell and V_precharge, and third multiply k by that initial difference. For example, with C_cell = 30 fF, C_BL = 300 fF, V_cell = 1.0 V, and V_precharge = 0.5 V, k = 0.0909 and ΔV_BL is only about 45.5 mV.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nBy charge conservation before the sense amplifier fires, total charge redistributes across (C_cell + C_BL), neglecting parasitics. Because C_BL is roughly 10x larger than C_cell, the resulting signal is only tens of millivolts. That explains why DRAM sense amplifiers need tight offset matching, quiet V_precharge (VDD/2), and clean sensing timing.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify bitline precharge/equalization timing (tRP)—if V_precharge hasn't settled, ΔV_initial shrinks asymmetrically for 1s vs. 0s.\n2. Check VDD and VPP (wordline boost) rails; low VPP increases access-transistor resistance and slows charge sharing.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.puny9iguska8-2","topic":"Fundamental Knowledge Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Fund Q3: What is the relationship between tRC, tRAS, and tRP in DRAM row timing, and why does it matter?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nRow-cycle time tRC must be at least tRAS plus tRP. tRAS is the minimum row-active time—how long the wordline must stay open so the sense amplifiers finish restoring charge into the cell. tRP is the minimum row-precharge time needed to close the row and equalize the bitlines back to VDD/2 before the next activation.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nFor example, if tRAS = 32 ns and tRP = 14 ns, then tRC must be at least 46 ns (exact numbers come from the JEDEC/device datasheet for the active speed bin). Violating tRAS cuts off charge restoration before the cell reaches full voltage, causing weak-cell read failures later; violating tRP leaves bitlines unequally precharged, shrinking sensing margin on the next ACTIVATE.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Dump the memory controller timing registers for the active speed bin and compare tRAS, tRP, and tRC against the DRAM datasheet.\n2. Test with relaxed tRP/tRAS (or lower frequency bin) to see if bank-thrashing errors disappear.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.puny9iguska8-3","topic":"Fundamental Knowledge Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Fund Q4: Why do sudden current transients (like ZQCal or burst compute) cause voltage droop, and what equation governs it?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nVoltage droop from fast current steps is governed primarily by package and PCB PDN inductance multiplied by the current slew rate (V_L = L × dI/dt), plus resistive IR drop. Even a few hundred picohenries of loop inductance multiplied by a steep dI/dt creates tens of millivolts of instantaneous droop before the VRM can even respond.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA VRM's control loop operates in tens to hundreds of kilohertz (microseconds), whereas silicon current steps happen in nanoseconds (dI/dt in A/ns). Local on-die and package decoupling capacitors must supply that fast charge via I_C = C × (dV/dt) while package/via inductance L opposes sudden current change. On an oscilloscope, this shows up as a sharp negative spike followed by damped ringing.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Start with a rated power-rail probe, DC coupling and suitable offset to preserve absolute voltage and slow droop. Choose bandwidth and scale for the event and record them. AC coupling can help inspect ripple but removes DC and changes low-frequency response.\n2. Trigger on the negative droop edge and correlate its timing against workload bursts or ZQCal commands.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.puny9iguska8-4","topic":"Fundamental Knowledge Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Fund Q5: How do τ = R × C and I_C = C × (dV/dt) help you debug slow signal edges or power-rail ramp failures?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nIf a rail ramps too slowly or a bus eye closes horizontally, excess C or series R is usually the culprit.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nOn a high-speed interface or I2C/reset line, excessive trace/pin capacitance or a wrong pull-up resistor inflates τ, causing edges to miss setup/hold windows or power-good timeouts. On power rails, ramping dV/dt too fast into huge bulk capacitance draws high inrush current, which can trip over-current protection (OCP) during power-on.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Measure 10%–90% rise/fall time on the oscilloscope and compare against 2.2 × R × C expected from schematic values.\n2. On power-up OCP trips, measure dV/dt ramp rate and calculate inrush current from total board capacitance.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.puny9iguska8-5","topic":"Fundamental Knowledge Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Fund Q6: What causes ringing on a power rail or clock/data trace, and how does the LC resonant frequency equation help you find the root cause?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nRinging happens when parasitic inductance L and decoupling/load capacitance C form an underdamped tank circuit that oscillates at its natural LC resonant frequency after a fast switching edge. The ringing frequency helps constrain possible L and C combinations, but cannot uniquely locate the source. Compare probe configuration, measurement position, load conditions and the PDN model before attributing it to a package, board or regulator.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nEvery PDN has parallel anti-resonance peaks where PCB/package inductance resonates with decoupling capacitors. Also, a sloppy oscilloscope measurement with a long 15 cm alligator ground lead adds ~100+ nH of probe-loop inductance, creating fake ringing around 50–150 MHz with the probe tip capacitance—which is why verifying with a short ground spring is mandatory before blaming the board.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Measure the ringing period T on the scope (f0 = 1/T) using a short < 5 mm ground spring (never a long ground clip).\n2. Re-measure with a second probe location to confirm the frequency is real board/package resonance, not probe-loop inductance.","source":"#"},{"id":"t.puny9iguska8-6","topic":"Fundamental Knowledge Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Fund Q7: Explain the transmission-line reflection coefficient Γ and what happens at an open, short, or matched load.","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nThe reflection coefficient Γ equals (Z_L − Z0) divided by (Z_L + Z0), giving the fraction of a signal wave reflected back from a load impedance Z_L on a trace of characteristic impedance Z0. When Z_L matches Z0, Γ = 0 (no reflection). At an open circuit, Γ = +1 (full positive reflection, doubling voltage). At a short, Γ = −1 (full inverted reflection).\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nAt high frequencies (PCIe, DDR/LPDDR DQ/CK), PCB traces behave as transmission lines rather than lumped wires. Any impedance discontinuity—wrong ODT (On-Die Termination), missing termination resistor, via stub, or bad socket pin—creates non-zero Γ. Reflected energy bounces back and forth, causing overshoot, undershoot, non-monotonic edges (ledges), and inter-symbol interference (ISI) that closes the data eye.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Look for stepped/ledge edges or severe overshoot/undershoot on the scope at the receiver.\n2. Verify ODT/ZQ calibration settings (PCODE/NCODE) and trace termination resistors against Z0 (e.g., 50 Ω single-ended, 85–100 Ω differential).","source":"#"},{"id":"t.puny9iguska8-7","topic":"Fundamental Knowledge Q&A — Translated & Expanded","question":"Fund Q8: How do you measure rail current and instantaneous power safely on a live bench using a calibrated sense resistor?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nUsing a differential probe (or matched Kelvin sense points) across a known milliohm R_sense preserves PDN integrity and gives MHz-bandwidth current waveforms on the oscilloscope, whereas a DMM only gives slow RMS/DC averages.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nUsing a differential probe (or matched Kelvin sense points) across a known milliohm R_sense preserves PDN integrity and gives MHz-bandwidth current waveforms on the oscilloscope, whereas a DMM only gives slow RMS/DC averages.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Verify the exact R_sense value (e.g., 1 mΩ–10 mΩ) from the board schematic/BOM and use Kelvin sense pads.\n2. Prefer a differential probe with adequate common-mode rating, rejection and bandwidth. If using two ground-referenced channels and subtraction, connect both ground leads only to the same permitted circuit ground; never place the ground clips on opposite sides of a high-side shunt. Subtraction does not provide isolation.\n3. Compute P(t) = V(t) × (V_sense(t) / R_sense) in scope math to capture peak transient power.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-01","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you stream a large log in Python?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nOpen the file and iterate one line at a time.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA file iterator avoids materializing the full log in RAM. A context manager closes the handle reliably.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-02","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you count error keywords in a log?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nNormalize each line, then increment a counter on a matching pattern.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nUse a compiled regex for multiple error signatures. Keep counts per message type and preserve the first timestamp.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-03","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Why use try/except around instrument reads?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nCatch expected transport failures and record them as errors.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nTime-outs and disconnected devices are expected fault classes. Handle only known exceptions and propagate unexpected ones.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-04","topic":"CODING QA","question":"What is the purpose of a finally block?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nRelease resources even when an operation raises an exception.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nIt runs for ordinary exception unwinding, but cannot guarantee cleanup after power loss or process termination.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-05","topic":"CODING QA","question":"When are retries safe in hardware control?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nOnly after checking whether the previous action actually occurred.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA repeated reset, toggle or flash can be harmful. Prefer idempotent operations, bounded retries and state reconciliation.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-06","topic":"CODING QA","question":"What is the difference between shallow and deep copying?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nShallow copies share nested objects; deep copies recursively duplicate them.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCopying a list of dictionaries shallowly can allow later edits to alter both structures. Test with nested values.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-07","topic":"CODING QA","question":"When would you use a lambda in Python?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nFor a small anonymous function used in a simple expression.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nFor example, key=lambda row: row['time'] sorts a list of records by timestamp.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-08","topic":"CODING QA","question":"What is a Python decorator used for?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nTo wrap a function with reusable behavior.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nInstrumentation can add logging, elapsed-time measurement or safe retry policies while preserving the core function.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-09","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Threads or processes for CPU-heavy analysis?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nUse processes when Python CPU-bound work is limited by the GIL.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nThreads can help overlapping blocking I/O; processes allow CPU-bound execution across cores at IPC overhead.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-10","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you run a CLI test from Python safely?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nUse subprocess.run with an argument list and a timeout.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCapture return code, stdout, stderr and elapsed time. Avoid shell=True for untrusted inputs.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-11","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Does a subprocess exit code of zero prove a test passed?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nNo. Validate completion, coverage and the tool's structured result.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA wrapper might exit zero despite internal failures. Inspect raw output and verify expected test counts.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-12","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you parse timestamped UART lines?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nRead lines incrementally, normalize timestamps, and classify events.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCapture boot stage, first error and reset reason while preserving raw output in chronological order.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-13","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How should serial communication timeouts be handled?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nUse bounded reads and report timeout with device and command context.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nDistinguish no response from a valid zero; close the connection safely and preserve partial data.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-14","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you test code without a real instrument?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nInject a mock device with deterministic responses and failures.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nUse unittest.mock or a fake instrument class to test both success and timeout paths.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-15","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How would you verify a log parser?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nCreate fixture logs with known pass, fail and malformed cases.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nCheck exact expected counts and failure directories, and run regression tests after parser changes.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-16","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you compute rail power from CSV columns?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nAlign voltage and current samples, then compute P=V×I.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nVerify units and timestamps; interpolating misaligned time series can conceal peaks or invent correlations.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-17","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you handle malformed numeric CSV cells?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nReject or flag invalid rows instead of treating them as zero.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nUse explicit conversion and provenance-aware error reporting; zero can be a legitimate measurement.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-18","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you detect outliers in voltage samples?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nCompare each sample against defined limits or a robust baseline.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nA median absolute deviation can resist outliers, but engineering acceptance limits come from specifications.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-19","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you use logging levels in an automated test?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nUse DEBUG for details, INFO for milestones and ERROR for failures.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nLogs should include timestamps, run ID, DUT configuration, command and meaningful exception context.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-20","topic":"CODING QA","question":"Why use a virtual environment for test scripts?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nTo isolate Python dependencies and pin reproducible versions.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nRecord the package versions and supported Python runtime alongside any test output.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-21","topic":"CODING QA","question":"What is the difference between assert and explicit validation?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nAssertions are development checks; explicit validation handles runtime errors.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nPython assertions can be disabled with -O, so safety checks and input validation should raise exceptions.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"},{"id":"public-code-22","topic":"CODING QA","question":"How do you write a minimal test for a shmoo parser?","answer":"WHAT I WOULD SAY · Core idea\nFeed a tiny synthetic grid with pass, fail and untested cells.\n\nDEEP TECHNICAL EXPLANATION · General mechanism\nVerify counts and boundary labels; never silently turn untested measurements into passes.\n\nBABY-STEP DEBUG / EVIDENCE · Practice workflow\n1. Identify inputs and expected output.\n2. Test a normal case and a fault case.\n3. Inspect the result, then rerun the same test after a correction.","source":"#"}];
